nsion and Transfer Guidelines Employment Forms and Letter Templates University Compensation and Classification Contact Us Careers My Career at Iowa Career Development Advising Services Job Search Tools Employment Categories and Classifications Professional and Scientific Positions Merit Employment Information Merit Supervisory Exempt and Confidential Staff Student Employment Talent Acquisition Apply at Iowa Department Resources Onboarding Competencies Competency Development Proficiency Levels Reviewing Competencies Universal Competencies Dual Career Services Development Meet The OE Team Professional Development and Training My Training Training Series For-Fee Workshops Learn Anytime Tuition and Fee Assistance Leading and Supervising Coaching Leadership Programs Leadership Anytime Mentorship Principles Create Your Leadership Plan Consultations & Support Org Development Toolkit Consultation Process Supervisor Training & Toolbox Reward and Recognition Programs Types of Recognition Reward and Recognition: Supervisor Guide Campus Awards Improving Our Workplace Award (I.O.W.A.) Celebrations on Campus Give a Hawkeye High-Five Performance Management Performance Reviews Addressing Performance Concerns Building a High-Performing Team Goal Setting liveWELL Work-Life Family Services Workplace Flexibility Financial Well-Being UI Wellness Health and Well-Being Fair Personal Health Assessment Health Coach Services Recreation Membership Incentive Program Programs Departmental Toolbox Resource Library liveWELL News Employee Assistance Program R U OK? Schedule an Appointment Doctor on Demand and Community Providers UI Emergency Hardship Fund Suicide Prevention Support for Supervisors Presentation Topics Work-Life Resources Campus Support and Critical Incident Response Ergonomics Program Computer Ergonomics Ergonomics Education Non-Computer Ergonomics At-Home Ergonomics Safe Patient Handling Remote Work Ergonomic Toolkit Support I-9 Information Faculty and Staff Disability Services Family and Medical Leave Act (FMLA) Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) Parental Leave Pregnant Worker Fairness Act (PWFA) Unpaid Medical Leave UI Health Care and CCOM Leave and Disability Requests Employee Relations Workplace Notices Reduction in Force and Layoffs Social Media Usage Work Rules Faculty and Staff Immigration Services Labor/Union Relations AFSCME Public Safety Bargaining Unit Staff Nurses and Allied Health Professionals (SEIU) Graduate Teaching and Research Assistants (COGS) Weingarten Rights for Employees Recertification Elections Layoff Resources How Layoffs Work Resources for Finding a New Job What Happens to Benefits and Pay Coping with Loss, Change, and Uncertainty WARN Act Workers' Compensation Treatment Locations Process Prevention and Return to Work Forms Glossary Resources Administrative Services Strategy and Reporting HR Roadmap Year in Review HR Benchmarking Toolbox Campus HR Community Enterprise Summit Senior HR Leadership Council University HR Staff Recognition Programs Working at Iowa survey Discover Your University Employee Resources Benefits Employee Benefits Employee Discount Program Retirement Plans Vacation and Sick Leave Well-Being Resources Employee Assistance Program Ergonomics Program Family Services liveWELL Workplace Flexibility Pay Pay Plans Payroll Services Position Classifications Time Records Onboarding Staff Onboarding Faculty Onboarding I-9 Information Working at a Public University Community Expectations Reward and Recognition Programs Give a Hawkeye High-Five Professional Development Workers' Compensation Policies Annual Policy Notifications Chicago native Ashley Price, BSN, RN, PMH-BC, has appreciated a culture at UI Health Care that is built on collaboration, kindness, and a shared sense of mission. Breadcrumb Home News A place – and a job – that feel like home: One nurse's journey from the suburbs of Chicago to Iowa City Ashley Price found more than a job at UI Health Care – she found a community that has allowed her to grow, both at work and at home. Tuesday, March 31, 2026 Written by UI Health Care Ashley Price, BSN, RN, PMH-BC, thought she was heading home for good after nursing school. But once she returned to the bustle of her hometown in Chicago's northern suburbs to complete her licensing exams, she realized something surprising – she missed Iowa. She missed the people, her family connections, and the pace of life she'd come to appreciate during her time at Luther College in Decorah, Iowa. So, she made the decision to return to Iowa to build her career. Here, she joined University of Iowa Health Care and found far more than a job. She found a community that welcomed her with open arms and invested in her success from the start. \"From the moment I arrived, I felt like I belonged,\" Price says. Over the past decade, UI Health Care – and the Iowa City area – have been the perfect places for Price as her life has evolved, both at work and at home. A career shaped by unexpected opportunities Price began her career at UI Health Care in inpatient child psychiatry before transitioning to adult psychiatric care, eventually stepping into her current position as an assistant nurse manager. Over the years, UI Health Care has offered her opportunities she never expected. From leadership roles to participation in an American Nurses Credentialing Center (ANCC) National Magnet & Pathway Conference to flexible scheduling that allowed her to teach clinicals at nearby Kirkwood Community College, Price says it all helped shape the leader she is today. \"What has always impressed me is the commitment to support at every level. Leaders are visible, engaged, and genuinely invested in nurturing their teams,\" Price says. Having worked with many leaders who encouraged her own growth, Price says she feels grateful to now mentor her team members as they navigate their career paths. Price says the culture at UI Health Care is built on collaboration, kindness, and a shared sense of mission. And as part of Iowa's only academic medical center, nurses are encouraged to use the full breadth of their training, contribute ideas, engage with research, and help shape the future of patient care. Together, those experiences have provided the right balance of leading-edge innovation and supportive work culture for Price. \"Here [at UI Health Care], it's the best of all worlds. Endless opportunities for career growth and education, coupled with a quality of life that can't be beat.\" Ashley Price, BSN, RN, PMH-BC A sense of community at work and at home While her responsibilities at work have expanded, so has her life at home. Price went from being single and living in a condo to married with a young son living in a smaller nearby town. The sense of belonging she feels at her job extends beyond her workplace – it's part of her community, too, providing a quality of life she didn't expect. Price and her husband now live in North Liberty, a quick 15-minute drive from UI Health Care's university campus and a far cry from the long commutes of large cities. It also gives their 4-year-old son the chance to thrive through sports, school, and a safe neighborhood filled with young families. The family spends their free time exploring local parks and hiking trails, attending Iowa Hawkeye football games, swimming at Lake Macbride State Park , and enjoying the variety of restaurants and cuisines in the Iowa City area. \"There is just so much more to do here than people realize,\" Price says. When they want a change of pace, Price says they will plan weekend getaways in historic cities like Dubuque (90 minutes away), in the capital city of Des Moines (1 hour, 45 minutes away), or back home to Chicago (3 hours, 30 minutes away). The best of all worlds Today, Price encourages other nurses – especially those from urban areas who want to grow their careers and families – to consider UI Health Care and the Iowa City area. UI Health Care has recently expanded from one hospital to three, so there are even more opportunities for nurses to find a role that fits their career goals and lifestyle needs. \"Here, it's the best of all worlds. Endless opportunities for career growth and education, coupled with a quality of life that can't be beat,\" Price says. \"I came for my career, but I stayed because this community has given me and my family so much more than I ever expected.\" Interested in joining our nursing team? If you want to work with extraordinary nurses in an exciting and challenging environment, please consider what the Iowa difference can do for you and your career. We offer many career opportunities for talented nurses eager to make their mark. Related content Third-generation nurse thrives within 'culture of support' Wednesday, January 8, 2025 Written by UI Health Care As a telemetry nurse, Wells and his unit are responsible for monitoring heart rhythms and oxygen levels using sensors that collect data and transmit it back to equipment that can read it. At UI Health Care, he's experienced strong leadership and teammates who care about each other. Meet the mother-daughter pair leading two generations of nursing with UI Health Care Thursday, June 5, 2025 Written by UI Health Care Jeanie Finnegan and her daughter Ashley have both gone beyond their job descriptions to grow in their careers and serve patients at UI Health Care. Campus recreation helps propel physics professor to international track success Wednesday, July 9, 2025 Written by Elizabeth Ireland John Prineas, a professor in the Department of Physics and Astronomy, trains seven days a week, alternating between outdoor runs and strength training at the Campus Recreation and Wellness Center. The University of Iowa University Human Resources University Services Building (USB) 1 W. Prentiss Street Iowa City, Iowa 52242 Benefits: 319-335-2676 | benefits@uiowa.edu Payroll: 319-335-2381 | payroll-services@uiowa.edu Admin Services: 319-335-3558 | univhr-admin@uiowa.edu Accessibility notice an